Original commit body from e7073925659b828c2f4ff412474a137f1746dc31:

MEASURED DEFECT. A PHP file containing
`file_put_contents("/tmp/out.txt", $_GET['payload'])` -- a textbook leak --
returned verdict `confirmed` at exit 0 for the claim "untrusted input must not
reach the filesystem", because PHP has no taint catalogue and "found no flows"
was reported as "no flows exist". A Kotlin file reading a socket and writing it
to disk did the same.

The rule already existed for BOUNDARY claims (WI-kajil / INV-bitig: a blind
analysis yields `inconclusive`, never a silent confirm). Taint claims skipped
it. INV-javam's signal was computed, and printed to stderr as a note asking the
READER to apply the downgrade by hand -- "Treat 'confirmed' verdicts on these
languages as inconclusive" -- which no CI gate and no hurried human does. The
verdict word `inconclusive` and the exit code 2 both already existed.

ONE RULE, ONE PLACE. `_require_coverage_to_confirm` is applied at the exit of
`verify_claims` to EVERY constraint kind, rather than added as a third copy of
`if not coverage.complete` beside the two in `verify_claim`. A constraint kind
added later cannot ship unable to distinguish "looked and found nothing" from
"did not look". The blind-language half REUSES `compute_boundary_coverage`
rather than growing a second implementation of "did this language produce call
edges" -- that function is precisely why the boundary side never had this bug.
A `violated` verdict is never downgraded: finding something is trustworthy
regardless of coverage; it is the CLEAN result that depends on having looked.

THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GATE WAS WRONG AND THE SUITE CAUGHT IT. Blocking on
`unsupported_taint_languages` directly meant a repo containing one yaml, json
or markdown file -- i.e. every real repo, including one that merely keeps its
own claims file in-tree -- returned `inconclusive` forever, making `confirmed`
unreachable. A verdict that is always inconclusive is exactly as useless as one
that is always confirmed. The gate now keys on languages that produced CALL
EDGES: a language with no call structure has nothing for a taint flow to travel
through, so its missing catalogue says nothing about the claim. Caught by
test_taint_recall_corpus::test_python_source_without_any_sink_confirms; my own
control test missed it because its fixture was a lone .py file. Pinned by
test_a_data_file_in_a_covered_repo_does_not_block_confirmation.

VERIFIED LIVE, four cases:
  php leak (code, no catalogue)          -> inconclusive, exit 2   [was: confirmed]
  clean python repo containing yaml      -> confirmed              [not blanket-downgraded]
  encrypt-then-remove control            -> confirmed
  kotlin socket->file                    -> confirmed              [KNOWN GAP, below]

KNOWN GAP, PINNED AS A STRICT XFAIL RATHER THAN LEFT IN A COMMENT.
`test_language_with_a_token_call_edge_still_falsely_confirms`. The coverage
check asks "did this language produce ANY call edges", and one edge is enough
to look covered. The Kotlin fixture emits exactly one `calls` edge while every
external method call -- ~95% of its 93 catalogued sinks (WI-nasuf) -- is
missing, so it still confirms. Closing it needs a finer signal than "any", e.g.
the share of method-construct call edges the catalogue can match, which is a
measurement exercise rather than a predicate change.

A TEST THAT PASSED FOR THE WRONG REASON, now fixed rather than left:
`test_verify_claims_notice_for_unsupported_taint_language` has no edges in its
fixture, so it goes inconclusive down the VACUOUS-ANALYSIS path; deleting the
uncatalogued-language rule outright would leave it green. The new
`test_uncatalogued_language_WITH_code_blocks_confirmation` puts a code-bearing
uncatalogued language beside a covered one so only the rule under test can
produce the verdict, and asserts the output names WHICH language was blind.

Two fixtures that asserted a VACUOUS analysis (zero call edges) could confirm a
taint claim were corrected to describe a repo the analysis actually looked at --
the same INV-bitig reason a boundary claim never could. Their real property (no
false notice on a supported language) is unchanged.

